---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../../styles/chart-compare.css';
import ChartCompare from '../../../islands/ChartCompare';
import candidate from '../../../data/platform-engine-candidate.json';

const title = 'Compare Two Chart Calculations | Zodiacs.org';
const description = 'Load two calculation records and see what differs between them, and how much of it can be explained. Runs in your browser; nothing is uploaded.';
---

<!--
  privateSurface: this page reads whole birth records out of imported files, so
  it opts out of the site-wide analytics scripts the way /profile/ and /ask/ do.
  Neither script ever saw chart data — they are page-level measurement — but a
  page whose whole claim is that your records never leave the device should not
  be the one page in that category still calling a third-party origin.
-->
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/developers/compare/" clientUi privateSurface>
  <section class="section container container--narrow">
    <em class="kicker">Developer tools</em>
    <h1 class="display">Why do these two charts differ?</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Two calculations of the same birth chart can disagree. Usually the reason is written
        down in the records themselves — a different house system, a different moment, a
        different convention — and reading it off is faster than arguing about it. This page
        does that reading, in your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        It reads the calculation records this site produces: download one from a{' '}
        <a href="/birth-chart/">birth chart</a>, let the{' '}
        <a href="/developers/examples/">developer starter</a> write one, or ask the{' '}
        <a href="/developers/mcp/">local MCP server</a> for <code>output: "record"</code>{' '}
        and keep the <code>record</code> field of its reply — that field is the record,
        and the reply around it is not one. They are{' '}
        <code>zodiacs.natal-envelope.draft-v1</code>, a Zodiacs-owned draft vocabulary rather
        than an industry interchange format — so another program's export and a screenshot
        are both out of scope, and the four examples below are the fastest way to see what an
        answer looks like.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ChartCompare client:load />

    <h2 id="how-it-decides">How it decides</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Differences come first, because they are facts: both files are read, and every value
        that disagrees is listed — inputs, conventions, angles, house cusps, every field of
        every body, and the aspect list. Longitudes are compared around the circle, so 359° and
        1° are two degrees apart rather than 358. Two numbers that print the same at the six
        decimals this page shows are called a rounding difference rather than a different
        calculation; the test is what they print, not a tolerance, because rounding is a step
        and no tolerance sits exactly on it.
      </p>
      <p>Explanations are then labelled by the evidence behind them:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reproduced</strong> — recalculated on this device, changing one setting and
          leaving everything else alone, and the result matched.</li>
        <li><strong>Reported</strong> — stated by the files themselves, with nothing to compute.</li>
        <li><strong>Hypothesis</strong> — fits the evidence, not demonstrated. Several
          hypotheses can fit one difference, and all of them are listed.</li>
        <li><strong>Unresolved</strong> — nothing in either file accounts for it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A recalculation only ever runs on engine <code>{candidate.version}</code>, the version
        this page carries. If a record was produced by a different version it is not re-run and
        presented as the original: the difference stays a hypothesis and the limit is stated.
        A version, checksum or source URL inside an imported file is a claim that file makes
        about itself, not proof that it is genuine.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reproduced is withheld unless the recalculation demonstrated the values that actually
        moved. Two polar charts that both fell back to whole sign have the same cusps however
        they were requested, and matching values neither file disputes demonstrates nothing.
        Only the house system is re-run: a different moment or place stays a hypothesis. Rows
        left over once each candidate is matched to what it can account for are listed as
        unresolved, by name.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both sides of a comparison usually come from the same engine. Agreement between two runs
        of one engine is consistency, not an independent check of astronomical accuracy.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="formats">What it reads, and what it does not</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Supported: the calculation record this site offers for download from a birth chart, and
        the records the <a href="/developers/examples/">developer starter</a> produces. Both are{' '}
        <code>zodiacs.natal-envelope.draft-v1</code>, validated with the engine's own parser and
        its size, depth and node limits. A record from a different engine version of that same
        schema is read, and the version difference is reported.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not supported, and not silently reinterpreted as something else: charts from other
        software in their own formats, PDFs, images and screenshots. There is no claim of
        general third-party compatibility here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your records never leave the device. The files are read in the browser, compared in
        memory and dropped when you clear them or close the page. No account, no upload, no
        birth details in the address bar, and nothing written to storage. The optional summary
        download leaves out dates, coordinates and positions but keeps the exact difference
        between them, so anyone holding one of the two charts can recover the other: safer to
        share than a full record, not anonymous.
      </p>
    </div>
    <h2 id="other-software">The engine beside other software</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This page compares two records that Zodiacs software wrote. How the engine and its API
        compare with other astrology software is in the developer guides, which give the other
        projects' versions and licences as their publishers state them:{' '}
        <a href="/developers/docs/swiss-ephemeris-alternative-javascript/">Swiss Ephemeris alternatives in JavaScript</a>,{' '}
        <a href="/developers/docs/ephemeris-npm/">Ephemeris packages on npm</a>,{' '}
        <a href="/developers/docs/python-astrology-library/">Python astrology libraries</a> and{' '}
        <a href="/developers/docs/astrology-api/">Astrology API</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
